[{:seon.task/name "a-call-with-no-arguments-to-no-var-is-told"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-07 by breaking src under `seon.main-test/every-name-a-form-uses-is-a-var`. `(seon.config/nope)`, a call with no arguments, compiles as a class's static member: the form's :form/refs holds ``jolt.host/static-member``, not the name, so `seon.program/unknown-refs` never sees it, on the program or on an eval's answer. `(seon.config/nope 1)`, a bare ``seon.config/nope`` and `(map seon.config/nope [1])` are all recorded and told. Running it says \"No such var: seon.config/nope\", so a zero-argument misspelling still reaches main on a path no test runs, the class docs/resolution.md#a-name-with-no-var names. Reproduce: `(my.test/with (my.test/world) (my.test/code 'app.v \"(ns app.v (:require [seon.config]))\\n(defn f [] (seon.config/nope))\\n\"))` and read the form's :form/refs. Correct: the account records the name a static-member call names when its class part is a namespace this process holds (`seon.program/unit-account`'s walk, its :invoke case), so the one law and the one finding see it; a real static (`Long/MAX_VALUE`, `(System/nanoTime)`) stays no finding. Then break src with a zero-argument misspelling and watch the law go red."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/form-calling-misspelled-var-held-namespace-arguments-has}}
 {:seon.task/name "compiled-units-in-the-store"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: the owner reopens it, when point-in-time loading is measured as the bottleneck. Deferred, kept open (docs/decisions.md#compiled-units-are-not-stored-now-the-idea-stays-open, docs/storage.md#compiled-code): not to be released until reopened; its proof is what reopening must make green. Nothing compiles at an agent's fork (copy-on-write from the fork server, fork-ms p50 0.68 ms) or in a root boot beyond `seon.program/load-all!`'s catch-up of forms the binary lacks (the store-native paper of 2026-10-22 (git history)). A real form loads in 1.6-5 ms, ~3.6 ms of it Jolt's analysis and emit plus Chez's compile (what a stored unit would replace, by a 0.01 ms `load-compiled-from-port`) and 1-2 ms contract arming (which stays). A Chez unit is deterministic bytes, 1.7-2.6 KB for a 450 B form; Jolt's AOT units are 15x their source with absolute paths, and every Jolt or Chez pin bump strands them. Reopen when point-in-time loading is measured as the bottleneck: per-form units keyed by (form text, what it reaches, runtime fingerprint: the :seon.tx/runtime attribute every commit carries), after Jolt emits one form to a bytevector with no absolute paths. The larger cost on that path is macro fan-out: `seon.program/load!` of 67 seon.lib forms with their dependents took 2,367 ms against 109 ms for the forms alone (`seon.program/dependents`). The named proof is not written (no deftest of that name in test/seon/program_cost_test.clj)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-cost-test/loading-a-form-from-its-stored-unit-costs-its-arming}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-repl-at-a-commit"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: waits for the owner's go (stated below). A branch is rebuilt from its definitions; evaluations are history. Already true (the store-native paper of 2026-10-22 (git history)): `seon.program/load-all!`, `seon.program/load-changes!` and `seon.program/load-diff!` read live forms' :form/src, and nothing re-runs an evaluation. Missing: a REPL on the program as of commit C, made by forking the fork server's image, `d/commit-as-db` at C (0.7-0.9 ms) and `seon.program/load-diff!` to it (1.6-5 ms per differing form). It restores code and data defs exactly (main: 1,119 code forms, 87 literal and 56 computed defs); host cells (7 atoms/delays) come back empty and host objects (ffi libraries, LMDB, sockets, procs, threads) are made fresh, as at boot; an agent's REPL state outside definitions stays with `clone!`. Waits for the owner's go. Checked 2026-11-06: `d/commit-as-db` exists (seon.ctx reads a base commit with it), no var or op opens a REPL on the program at a commit, and the named proof is not written; the counts above are of 2026-10-22 (this tree's db holds 4,744 live forms, src and test). docs/turn.md#parked says starting and resuming at a commit is planned, not built."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-repl-at-a-commit-holds-that-commits-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "loading-a-changed-file-keeps-its-other-vars-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: a root started on target/seon built from src, then src/seon/program.clj edited (one defn removed, one docstring changed), with :seon.config.source/dirs [\"src\" \"test\"] (`seon.agent-test/in-agent`): in the agent (a fork of the fork server after the import), `(seon.program/graph-index ...)` answers \"Attempting to call unbound fn: #'seon.program/graph-index\", an unchanged var of the changed file, and `seon.agent/test-plan` selects nothing (four seon.agent-test tests red, one root hung in stop!). Rebuilt from the same tree, all are green. So loading a changed file's forms into a process whose binary holds the file (`seon.program/load-changes!`, `seon.fork-server/server-step` :tx, which runs every file's ns form first) leaves a var it did not change unbound. Cause to find, at its owner: the ns form's evaluation, `seon.program/remove-definition!` of the removed var, or the load of the changed form. Correct: a changed file loads its changed forms and leaves every other var as the binary holds it. Until fixed, bin/test builds target/seon before anything runs (docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches). The named proof is not written. Not reproduced on 2026-11-06 at a REPL of root on target/seon: `seon.source/refresh-tx` of src/seon/program.clj with one docstring changed, then `seon.program/load-changes!` of its ops (54, :tests? false): no errors, no var of seon.program unbound (the changed form was not loaded: `held?` counted it the binary's own); the removed-defn case and the in-agent fork were not tried."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/loading-a-changed-file-keeps-its-other-vars-bound}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-form-names-its-namespace"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-24 (worktree session store, under seon.db/a-killed-root-recovers-main-in-the-durable-read-check). Measured on a store with no example program (durable selected alone through bin/check --steps, which then gave it no copy of the seeded store; a directory holding only steps now gets one: docs/checks.md#running-steps-by-hand): an agent's (in-ns 'app.m0) (defn f1 ...) and (in-ns 'app.m1) (defn f2 ...), merged to main, are on main as two live forms whose :form/ns entity has no :ns/name (main holds no :ns/name datom at all), so seon.program/live-forms reads :ns nil; seon.program/load-all! on that db answers {:loaded 2 :errors ()} and afterwards (find-ns 'app.m0) is nil, and every agent after the restart answers No such namespace: app.m0. seon.program/account-tx does assert {:ns/name n} for an account's namespace, so the name is lost after it. The merge's half is fixed (12f682039d, 2026-10-27: seon.merge carries the namespace entity of a form it carries; proofs seon.merge-test/a-merge-carries-the-namespace-of-a-form-it-carries and a-namespace-both-sides-made-is-one-entity-after-the-merge). Still true on 2026-11-06, at root's REPL on a db of two forms seon.program/record-tx recorded in app.m0, the :ns/name datom of app.m0 retracted: seon.program/live-forms reads :ns nil for them, seon.program/load-all! answers :errors [] and (find-ns 'app.m0) is nil. Correct: a merged form loads into its namespace after any restart (the merge keeps the name now), and a form with no namespace is an error of the load, not a quiet load. Proof: seon.program-test/a-merged-form-loads-into-its-namespace-after-a-restart (to write): a form made under in-ns on a store with no such namespace, merged, the program reloaded in a fresh process, finds the var. Replaces nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-merged-form-loads-into-its-namespace-after-a-restart}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-kept-trees-program-is-its-trees-import"
  :seon.task/plan "Defect (seen 2026-10-24 on jolt/agent-conversation): a partial branch in the kept program store spreads to every tree built from it. `test-runner/program-db` keeps one branch per git tree (:tree-<id>) in :seon.config.proofs/program-store; a tree never seen is branched from the kept tree with the fewest changed program files and brought current by `seon.source/refresh-tx` over only those files, so nothing ever checks a kept branch against its tree, and a branch missing namespaces passes the loss to each tree near it. Reproduction as far as known: on jolt/agent-conversation, `bin/test --plan both DIR` at aae52f09e, 201f8f1ce and cd330af55 (cd330af55 differs from 61b4d2c9d only in notes and docs) saw 43-67 tests and named every seon.check step as defining no function, against 651 tests and 37 steps at 61b4d2c9d and 650 at main's d92b8819a; after `seon.db/kept-store!` removed the store (a schema change) and it was rebuilt, cd330af55 read 651/37. Seen again 2026-10-25 on jolt/agent-conversation at dedb66602 (after the merge of main f83b736e1): `bin/prove`'s plan named every seon.check step as defining no function (\"check steps naming a function the program does not define\": all of them but laws, health and lmdb-kill) and failed; the store (target/program-store-2c479bc6) had been made fresh at 08:12 that morning after an earlier deletion, during a stretch where a test file did not read (a duplicate :response key in a map literal in test/seon/render_test.clj, fixed within the hour), so a branch made from that import kept a short program and each later tree refreshed from it; deleting the store and planning again read 691 tests and 37 steps at the same tree. So a file that fails to read at import was one way a branch is made short; since then `test-runner/program-db` makes a tree's facts before its branch and a refused transaction takes the branch with it, so that way is closed (its docstring), and the other is unknown. How the first partial branch was made is unknown: the store holding it is gone. Leads: refresh-tx re-reads a moved namespace's dependents through `seon.source/file-text`, which may answer nil for a test file; `test-runner/tree-files` given no changed paths lists and archives the whole repository (`git ls-tree -r` with no path) though it reads nothing. Correct: a kept tree's program equals the import of its tree (the same namespaces and forms), checked where the branch is made at the cost of the changed files (each namespace of the tree's file list present), never only at the next plan; a branch found short is made again from import-tx, and says so. Replaces nothing; the check is in program-db, the store's one writer. Proofs: seon.proofs-test/a-kept-trees-program-is-its-trees-import (to write: a kept branch made short, then a plan of a tree branched from it, holds every namespace of its tree), seon.proofs-test/a-kept-tree-costs-its-lookup-not-its-program (exists: the check keeps the lookup's cost)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-kept-trees-program-is-its-trees-import
                      seon.proofs-test/a-kept-tree-costs-its-lookup-not-its-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-older-branch-runs-on-a-newer-binary"
  :seon.task/plan "Found on jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-24, scripted runs dbg/near#2, #3 and dbg/fold#3 on its worktree's root): a task whose branch forked before the binary's schemas changed cannot start a run. seon.turn/work! throws seon.schema.datahike/declarations invalid-input, the registry it was given being {} (expected :seon.host/registry), and the run closes :no-end. The branch's older schema forms read as changes against the binary (seon.program/schema-changes), and the registry made from them (seon.program/schema-registry) reaches seon.program/schema-declarations or seon.db's declarations as {}. Since 7f99615900 (2026-10-27) schema-registry answers the built registry (seon.contract/registry), not {}, when nothing changed (seon.program-test/a-program-with-no-schema-change-declares-nothing-new), so the {} is gone for a merge with no schema change; whether a turn on a branch with an older schema form still fails is not re-run (it needs a run of an agent). A fresh task forked from the current main runs. Correct: a branch older than the binary runs, its forms in effect (or its schema changes merged onto the binary's, as a merge does); schema-registry never answers a value its readers refuse. Proof to write: an agent on a branch with an older schema form starts its run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/an-older-branch-runs-on-a-newer-binary}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-contract-check-does-not-reach-every-render"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 after jolt/agent-conversation merged (1ea5bc781): bin/prove planned 29 keys of 686 tests and left 662 out as reaching a process, so a green bin/prove ran almost nothing. The edge that tipped it, seon.render.ui/did naming my.agent/prove as a quoted symbol, is fixed (jolt/a-test-reaches-what-it-calls; proof seon.render-test/a-render-reaches-no-process). The cause it exposed stays: every test reaches every render. A contracted function's form refers to the registry (seon.contract/checked, seon.contract/registry, seon.config/schema-forms), the registry holds every schema entry, and a schema entry names its renders (:seon.render/hiccup, :seon.render/ai: seon.render.ui/agent, seon.render.ai/system and the rest), so the reach walk (seon.program/reach-graph: a schema entry's names are its neighbours) goes from any contracted call into the whole render layer. Measured at the fix: 247 tests and steps still left out, among them pure tests that start nothing (the render test that nothing is shown twice, the-screen-says-what-the-run-did, seon.ctx.wire-test/each-thing-is-said-once); and any change to any render moves every test's digest, so every test reruns. Correct, at the producer of the edge: what a contract check reaches is the schema it checks (its Malli form and the entries that form names), not the renders a schema declares in its properties: a render is reached by what renders the value (seon.render's dispatch on :seon.render/hiccup, its callers), never by a validation. The reach graph's schema entries take a schema entry's form names as neighbours and leave out its :seon.render/* property values (they are calls by name only when rendered, which the render dispatch's own form reaches). Proof: on the real src program, the tests bin/prove leaves out are exactly those whose own forms (and the test helpers they call) reach a process var by a call path that does not go through a schema's render property; a pure render test (the render test that nothing is shown twice) is selected; and a change to one render moves only the digests of tests that render through it. Replaces nothing; changes the reach walk's schema entries (seon.program/form-row's :schema for a schema entry), its one producer. Checked 2026-11-06 on this tree's program (seon.program/reach-graph, graph-index, seon.fx/inputs): 1,438 tests, 205 of them :process?; seon.render-test/a-render-reaches-no-process and seon.ctx.wire-test/each-thing-is-said-once are not; 47 tests reach seon.render.ui/agent (seon.program/tests-reaching). Since 993e85485d (2026-10-31) seon.program/schema-row gives a schema entry the vars its properties name as edges, on purpose (seon.program-test/a-render-a-schema-entry-declares-is-in-its-tests-reach): the Correct above reverses that. The named proof is not written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/only-a-test-that-starts-a-process-is-left-out}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-definition-change-costs-what-it-changes"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-30 (md-scan #28's kept branch bench-28, the context session): an agent's eval allocated 47 MB at the median; the costliest ten were the platform's work on a definition change, never the agent's code: (my.schema/register! :seon.ref/block ...) 214-230 MB and ~25M ticks each (five of them, one per schema change), (seon.repl/edit! 'seon.ref/scan ...) 162-169 MB and ~21M ticks each. What a change does: records the form (seon.program/record-tx), re-arms the contracts naming a changed schema (seon.program/rearm!, seon.contract/rearm!), updates the reach graph (seon.agent's reach, seon.program/reach-update), and the turn's re-draw. A change of one definition should cost what that definition reaches, not the program's size. Plan: profile one register! and one edit! in an agent (seon.profile/measure!, touched by :self-bytes), name the costliest functions, and make each cost what the change touches (a cost proof per path, n = the program's size with one definition changed: flat). Also: those evals' records hold :fns empty, so the record cannot say where an eval's cost went: why (the record's costliest functions are taken where?), and fix it so every eval's record carries them."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/register-edit-single-definition-agent-cost-same-ticks}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-reading-mains-history-takes-its-head"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-30: seon.task-files-test/no-shipped-task-is-merged-work reads main's merge messages (git log through clojure.java.shell/sh, whose env class is :seon.env/bin only), so its verdict was reused across the merge whose message named a task: red on main from 15b53fa3ae until a later full run, while plain proves of the next branches said it was green. What it reads is main's history, not a file of the tree, so neither the tree digest nor a file class covers it. Correct: a test reading main's history has main's head commit in its digest (a class of its own, :seon.env/main-head, from seon.fx/inputs), or the check moves to where history is made: the merge itself refuses a message naming a task whose entry it leaves (bin/prove's merge line already names the merge's tasks). Decide which with the owner's rules on checks (a check that cannot run where it judges is in the wrong place). The named proof is not written, and no :seon.env/main-head exists in src (the classes are seon.fx/env-class's: system, web, deps, bin, schemas, src)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-test-reading-mains-history-reruns-when-main-moves}}
 {:seon.task/name "another-trees-contract-is-its-own"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-01: seon.program/account reads a definition's contract (contract-text) and meta (def-meta) from the live var first when it is bound, so a file analyzed without running (seon.source/accounts, :before nil: another tree's program, an old commit's, root's import of main) records this image's contract and meta for any var the image also defines, not its text's. The docstring had the same cause and now reads the form first (seon.program-test/a-file-analyzed-keeps-its-own-docstring). Why the contract is not changed the same way yet: contract-text prefers the live var's schema when it is plain data (a schema var a form names, resolved to its value), and the form's text alone would store the name unresolved, which moves the stored contract of every such var and every digest reaching it. Correct: for an analysis without running, the contract is resolved against the analyzed program (the tree's own schema var, or the db's), never the image; def-meta takes the compiler's account. Proof: a file analyzed beside an image whose var has another contract records the file's. Reproduced 2026-11-06 at root's REPL: (seon.source/accounts 'seon.program \"(defn schema-changes {:malli/schema [:=> [:cat :int] :int]} [x] x)\\n\") records :contract \"[:=> [:cat :seon.host/db] [:map-of :qualified-keyword :any]]\" (the image's), not the file's. The bug-class audit of 2026-11-03 said that when :before is nil account does not call find-var and that it answers a declared map; on 2026-11-06 account calls find-var for every def it makes (the var goes to contract-text and def-meta whatever :before is) and its contract answers [:maybe :map]. The named proof is not written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-file-analyzed-keeps-its-own-contract}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-top-level-form-with-no-var-is-in-a-digest"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-06: a defect put into the Scheme block of seon.profile (a bare top-level scm/eval-string, which defines no var; seon.profile/procs only names what it made) left every seon.profile-test digest unchanged while the tree moved, so bin/test ran nothing and reported green, though the same defect is red on a fresh cache. A change to a defn in the same file does move them. The same shape: ffi/load-library in seon.lib and seon.sock, add-watch and set-validator! in seon.ctx, the doseq in my.agent. What should be true: a form with no var is in the digest of whatever reaches its namespace, so a change to it reruns those tests. To look at first: seon.program/graph-index and :form/ns, whether a namespace's forms with no :form/defines can join a reaching var's digest at the cost of what changed. Proof: changing such a form moves a test's digest. Reproduced 2026-11-06 on my.test's world (seon.program/graph-index digest of a test calling f): ns app.q15 with (defn f [x] (inc x)) and a bare (println \"a\"): the digest is the same with the bare form (println \"b\") and moves when f's body changes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/changing-bare-top-level-form-defines-var-println}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tests-digest-reads-what-it-ran"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen twice on 2026-11-08: a process test's verdict misses the code run in the processes it boots or drives by text (ts/start-root!, ./seon CMD, code sent to root as a string): seon.root/start-agent changed under the root test of an agent's name across a restart (Context; now seon.root-test/a-second-boot-resumes-the-same-main) and seon.check/boot-twice under seon.root-test/a-second-boot-in-one-process-is-refused (a test agent) reused old greens, and a forced red stored at a green's digest read FLAKY. Those processes record their reads too (seon.profile/reads in each booted process, answered into the test's verdict), or the verdict says it read a process it cannot see. Owner, 2026-11-08: \"I don't understand how we don't know what tests ran? That sounds like a bug.\" It is: a process test's agent reaches root's code by messages to the server, which no static reach follows (docs/sharing.md#a-result-stands-until-what-it-read-changes). Owner, 2026-11-07: \"I want a tests digest to be the inputs (ideally just a seed) so yeah there is no need to rerun them if the seed is the same and so are the functions.\" A green stands while what its run read is unchanged, wherever it ran. Reads: a fixed seed from the test's name and a config salt (not from its digest, a nightly salt bump finds new cases); every contracted function it called (seon.profile/reads, in each verdict's :fns since 813786f9db, those a nested epoch hid at zero counts); what those reach through data (value defs, macros, schema entries, plain namespace forms, stopping at a function never called); its own namespace's forms; config keys read at seon.config/resolve-in and files read. The check: hash the current values of a test's last green's reads; none, or one changed, runs it. Reads are a union of recent runs. Each read is the digest of the form the process loaded, per var. A run using a definition with no program record keeps its verdict to its own process (Context: a value eval'd in, read through resolve, goes green and shared today). A walk of a registry, all-ns, a multimethod's table or a name's absence reads the whole set: additions are not seen by a list of calls, so seon.program/registry-walkers stays until a mechanism covers them. Reds keep reads and answer red as recorded, rerun when named; an agent reuses only its own. Evidence, in-process tests, one run each: replay of 17 merges, reruns 3,214 today, 325 by this rule, 250 by bare calls, median per merge 53, 2, 1 (an estimate), forms read 442, 108, 29; mutation of 1,500 unread functions over 60 tests, 5 red, all in 2 tests, all recorded since 813786f9db, and the method sees about 81 percent of real dependencies (95 of 117 controls); ran sets on the same code over 150 tests: same order 0 differ, first use in a process 4, order 2 (seon.sqlite/settle!). Order: experiments at a REPL; in-process tests' digest; process tests, whose children must report what they ran, the gate's about 7 minutes; only then remove the old rule and its lists. The bar for moving on is a regular fresh empty-cache full run on main finding no false green (reused greens hid 3 reds on main 2026-11-07), and that run stays. Web's experiments 7 and 8, by what a read would hash, not through the recorder: a function untouched on a user's branch reads as main's after a whole merge of a session and after a rebase, so digests share across both; one a branch redefined reads differently, so it takes its own verdict, and the rule needs nothing special; a test reading data on a user's branch directly is not covered. Correction: 813786f9db's message says the record's per-call cost read 0 ticks, but jolt.host/ticks counts only inside a measure, so that said nothing; measured properly a counted call is 7 ticks, the record included, not compared with a build before it. Context, from a real run: seon.render-test/a-render-is-opts-then-children kept one digest across a red and a green, because a render named only in a schema moved no digest; recorded reads should cover it, check when they exist. Open: experiment 9 additions, the check's cost, a scratch reused-by-reads answer for Context's experiment 5 (what an agent is told). Owners: Optimization this; Context status reads and real runs; Web digests across a merge and a rebase. One mechanism, not a parallel one: work's name, its reads, its result; when this holds, check steps and the gate's candidate keys move onto it, then compare with the render cache's d/with-reads, the most mature of the kind. Stopgaps this replaces, to delete with it: the hand-kept quoted symbol lists in seon.agent-test (`_ '[...]`, five tests, the newest a-real-gate-judges-a-candidate-once naming the gate code behind root's :gate message), which go stale silently when the path they name changes. A third case, in-process, 2026-11-10 (jolt/tests-cut): the Scheme text of `seon.profile` (the string its `procs` are built from) changed (seon-profile-alloc made process-wide) and seon.profile-test/a-threads-allocation-is-its-own ran red at the digest its green holds, so it read FLAKY: a test reaching seon.profile/alloc-bytes does not take the text its host procedures are compiled from."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/test-process-ran-code-through-string-sent-root}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-reaching-ctx-through-its-watch-has-no-edge"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-07: 60 non-process tests ran seon.ctx vars their reach does not hold (seon.ctx/on-state 45 tests, agent-record 35, install! 35), so a change to the ctx record's path moves none of their digests: a false green. Each runs them through what no reference shows; find what starts it (an atom watch installed in the agent) and give it an edge at its one site. The six runner vars also outside reach (seon.agent/test-report and the seon.sock writers) are the harness, not dependencies. Proof: a change to seon.ctx/on-state moves seon.ctx.wire-test/a-record-takes-the-agents-turn-in-force."
  :seon.task/kind :work
