 {:seon.task/name "a-namespace-defining-a-var-twice-is-red"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-10 (Context's 2b, reported by the managing session): Context's merge bea0f837d6 left two defns of seon.render.ui/agent-parts in src/seon/render/ui.clj; the later one silently redefined Web's, 36 tests went red, and no check was red for the cause. The class: a namespace that defines the same var twice passes every check, against AGENTS.md section 5 (\"Every definition happens once, where it is defined\"). What allowed it: the program import records each form's definitions (:form/defines) and a var's one :var/form, the last writer winning, and nothing compares a namespace's forms' definitions with each other. What correct looks like: an import (seon.program/record-tx and the seed's tx-of) that meets a second live form in one namespace defining a var another live form of that namespace defines is a problem of the namespace, red in bin/check health and refused at a merge, read from the import's facts (:form/defines per namespace), never a regex over source text. A form that replaces another (the same form edited, a defn moved within the file) is not a second definition: the old form is no longer live. Sweep on main f4968a8624: 0 duplicate top-level definitions in src and test (a count by a text scan, used only to size this; the check itself reads facts). Proof, red first: a world (my.test/world) with a namespace of two forms defining one var is red in the program's problems; one form, green. Merged from seon.source/a-var-defined-twice-in-a-file-is-said (2026-11-10 triage): same cause (a namespace or file defining a var twice passes every check); that task gains that both import paths (whole import and refresh-tx) keep the same one live form, the last by file order"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/world-namespace-has-two-live-forms-defining-var}}
{:seon.task/name "a-tests-reach-follows-the-components-its-render-calls"
  :seon.task/plan "Read 2026-11-10 (Web's case: a mutation of seon.render.ui/agent-parts reused the green of seon.ctx-test/a-join-added-to-the-agents-pattern-is-a-part-of-its-page; forced, it went red): a var literal in hiccup (#'agent-parts) IS a reference (checked: :form/refs holds it). The test reaches its page's render through seon.db/entity-index, which picks the entity schema at run time; a registry walker takes the schema files' text (:seon.env/schemas), never the code of the renders those entries name. Correct, first: a registry walker's form takes edges to every render a schema entry names (the schema rows' :refs vars), so its tests take the renders' code; cost: a render edit reruns the ~1,160 tests taking the schema class until recorded reads narrow it. Earlier finding: Found 2026-11-09 by Context in jolt/erase-window: seon.render-cost-test/a-restart-draws-its-turns-in-one-walk and drawing-latest-costs-one-request kept one digest each (d7e7e745f410e858, 4eb46df4e8d68238) across a change to seon.ctx/placed-messages, which the walk reaches at run time through a component var a render holds as data (the page's hiccup names components by var), so a red and a later green sat at one digest and read as flaky. Same class as the process entry (fixed 2026-11-09 by adding seon.repl/serve! to :seon.config.source/process-entry): a reach the static graph misses. Correct: the reach graph follows the component vars a render's hiccup or pattern names (a quoted var or symbol in head position of a component vector, as seon.render reads them), so a test that walks a page reaches what its components call. Never delete a red verdict to clear it (owner, 2026-11-09). Proof, red first: a test walking a page whose component calls f moves digest when f changes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
