 {:seon.task/name "a-tests-verdict-holds-the-schema-entries-it-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Recorded reads for in-process tests (owner, 2026-11-10: build it). What exists: a test's digest is static, its reach plus the input classes its reached forms name (seon.program/graph-index, env-class-readers). seon.db/entity-index chooses an entity schema at run time by the attributes an entity holds, so no static name says which entity schemas a test reads; since 19076dfbbe every test reaching it takes the whole :seon.env/schemas class: about 1,160 of 1,614 tests rerun on any entity-schema edit (599 before), and 196 of 757 landings from 2026-10-27 to 2026-11-10 touched the schema resources. The gate: (1) ours: render already records what a component read and keeps it per component (seon.render's :reads, d/reads-index, the readers index a transaction is matched against); verdicts are files keyed by digest (seon.fx/write-verdicts!), reused by seon.proofs/plan. (2) v1 selected by static reach only (seon.test, test_reaching_test), with no run-time record. (3) The field: Bazel's unused_inputs_list (an action names the inputs it did not read, and the next key drops them), ninja's depfiles (the compiler writes the headers it read; the next build keys on them), Salsa (each query records the queries it read and is revalidated by their revisions). (4) It adds one field to a verdict file, :reads {schema key content digest}, and a second reuse step; the static digest cannot hold it because the choice is made at run time. The recorder exists: seon.ratom's tracking view (defview: ILookup, IFn, Associative, Seqable over a value, each read notified by its key path, a missing key as a :shallow read, an iteration as a :keys read of its path), which is how a render knows what it read. Its readers (surveyed 2026-11-10) read the entity index by key, (es a), (:schema-identities ix) of a schema, required-keys of a touched schema, except seon.db/reaching (iterates :targets) and the write check's cascade (keys of :identity-schemas, only on a whole-entity retraction): those record a :keys read, which reads every entry, honestly. Plan: in a test child, the entity index each reader takes (seon.db/index, index-of) is that view over the value, the test run in a context that collects the reads, so each schema key resolved and each MISS (a key no schema holds) is recorded at the read, never at a cache fill (the second of two tests in one process reads through the first's cache). The verdict gains :reads. For an in-process test the digest drops :seon.env/schemas (the class of 19076dfbbe), and a verdict at that digest is reused only when every recorded key's current form digest equals the recorded one and no recorded miss now matches a schema; a verdict with no :reads (written before) reruns once. Process tests keep the class: their reads happen in other processes. Proved at n = 3 (2026-11-10, a scratch test, the binary's index swapped for a view and each test run in a context collecting reads): seon.problems-test/a-root-on-a-stale-binary-is-a-problem read 53 index paths, all by key (an attribute to its owning schema: a handful of entity schemas); seon.task-test/deleting-a-cited-var-is-refused-naming-its-cites read 54 by key plus the whole of :identity-schemas (the cascade on a whole-entity retraction, as surveyed); seon.problems-test/health-reads-each-part-once read none of the binary's index (it takes another: find which before building). The recorded path [:entity-schemas a] maps to the schema owning a, [:required-keys s] and [:forms s] to s, a :keys read of a top-level map to every entry. Estimates to measure before building past the recorder (stop if any is over 2x): tests moved by an edit of :seon.cite/cite-fact 150-250, of the agent's fact 300-500, of the eval's fact 250-400 (in-process only), against about 1,160 today. Cost line: the recorder is one set conj per lookup (n = lookups a test makes); the reuse check is one form-digest compare per recorded key, read from the schema entries once per plan. Proofs, red first: a test that reads only app.calc's forms is reused when an unrelated entity schema changes and reruns when a schema it matched changes; two tests in one process both record a schema the first one's lookup cached; a recorded miss reruns when a new schema matches it; a verdict without :reads reruns once. FOUND BY THE RECORDER (jolt/recorder f19af3b8ef, not landed, 2026-11-10): the index recorder works (read sets small: median 0-4 entity schemas a test over problems-, task-, db-, render- and ctx-test; a recorded lookup 345 ticks against 75-125), but (1) tests read the entity schemas OUTSIDE the index: Datahike's schema derived from them (seon.db/schema, under d/db-with and ts/test-db) and the contract registry (contract/properties); those tests record nothing, so a digest without the schema class on the strength of index reads alone lets a schema change through on a reused green, the false green this task exists to close; (2) a view read is two contracted calls, so call-count cost proofs go red under recording; (3) a value cached once per process (my.test/world) gives its reads only to its first taker. RE-PLAN (the managing session's go, 2026-11-10): record at the one choke point every reader of a schema form passes (seon.contract's lookup of a form in the registry), the db schema's derivation recorded as a read of the entity schemas it derives from; seon.cost measures with recording unbound; a cache keeps the reads it was filled with and replays them to each taker. THE RISK THAT DECIDES WHETHER TO BUILD IT: the derived Datahike schema is built from every entity schema, so if every test that transacts reads the whole derived schema, its read set is again every schema and the gain is gone. Then what to record is which attributes' schema a transaction actually consults (the attributes of its datoms, checked one by one), not the derivation. Before more is built: re-measure the read sets with the registry recorder on the same five namespaces, and count the tests that record every schema; if most do, record per attribute a transaction consults, or stop and say so. Wiki: docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache and docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach. Merged from seon.program/the-entity-index-readers-take-only-the-entity-schemas-that-can-match (2026-11-10 triage): same cause (the coarse :seon.env/schemas class on every test reaching the entity index); the recorded-reads task already states the finer sound input, so it gains the measurement-per-landing of class rule against finer rule and the proof that a test naming one entity's attributes keeps its digest under an unrelated schema edit"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/test-reads-only-app-calc-s-forms-reused}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-resumed-agent-binds-every-var-its-program-holds"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Found 2026-11-11 by the Data model session (reproduction, not chased): on its worktree .worktrees/inspect (jolt/inspect ab2f0fa5cf), with main holding my.schema as imported by my.root/import!, after bin/root stop and start a resumed agent's process has #'my.schema/uses interned and UNBOUND (calling it: \"Attempting to call unbound fn\") while #'my.schema/use-of, #'my.schema/links and #'my.db/pulled, imported the same way, are bound; (:load-errors @seon.agent/state) is []. In the first process after the import, before any restart, it was bound. uses is the last form of its file and the only one whose definition did not change in the last import. An unbound var with no load error is a silent miss. Likely place: the resumed agent's load of main's program (seon.agent/init!, program/load-diff! and the fork server's preload), which may skip a form whose text did not change while its var was interned anew; the sibling of a-merge-loaded-in-place-unbinds-what-it-deletes (the other direction). Correct: every var main's program defines is bound in a resumed agent, or its load error says why. Proof: a resumed agent over a store whose last import changed every form of a namespace but its last has that last var bound (red today by the reproduction)."
