 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-loaded-in-place-unbinds-what-it-deletes"
  :seon.task/plan "A var the merged tree deleted stays bound in a root that loaded the merge in place (docs/source.md#a-merge-loads-in-place, seon.program/load-changes!), and live code still calls it. Seen 2026-11-10 by Web on the standing root after batch 19 (main 1ff17f9ed0, which deletes seon.render.ui/users-side): root pid 51334, started 21:08 on batch 17's binary and given batch 19 by its landing, answered (resolve 'seon.render.ui/users-side) => a var and drew root's page with TWO agents lists, the user's (root-side, started-side) and the deleted users-side/earlier-sessions list, every agent twice; after a restart on 1ff17f9ed0 (pid 98124) the page drew one list. So load-changes! loads the forms a commit asserts but does not unmap the vars whose forms it retracts, and something in the running program still reached the old var (a caller not reloaded, or a render resolved by name). Correct: a merge loaded in place leaves the running program as a boot of the merged tree would (each var the tree no longer defines unmapped, each caller of it reloaded), so what a person sees after a landing equals what a restart shows. Owner: Optimization (load-changes!, the writer)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-merge-loaded-in-place-unbinds-a-deleted-var}}

 {:seon.task/name "a-test-reads-the-checkout-only-through-a-classed-reader"
  :seon.task/plan "Point's ruling 2026-11-11, on the docs reach (a 4-line wiki edit reran 117 tests): build only if the checkout-path rule shows its edge. What exists after that rule: a raw reader (```seon.program/tree-readers```: slurp, file-seq, io/resource) puts its test on the whole tree only in a form that names the checkout (seon.config/checkout) or passes a relative path; the wiki's and the task files' readers take their own file classes (```seon.program/env-class-readers```). Its unsound edge: a checkout path built in a helper the form calls without naming the checkout reads the checkout with no digest of it, which only the full run catches. Correct when that edge shows (a test reused over an edit to a file it read): a law that refuses a raw read of the checkout outside the classed readers (seon.doc/pages, seon.root/task-files, seon.fx/read-source), from the compiler's facts as the catch law reads them, so every checkout read has a class and its digest. Proof: a test form that slurps a checkout file through a helper is a problem row naming it."
